  "page1": {
    "title": "How Light Bends in a Lens",
    "subtitle": "Why a straw looks broken in water, and how lenses use this trick.",
    "body": "Look at a straw in a glass of water. It looks broken at the top. The straw is fine, but the light plays a trick.\n\nLight moves in a straight line through air. When light passes into water, it bends a little. We call this bending refraction.\n\nGlass can bend light too, and that is how a lens works. A lens is clear glass with curved sides. A convex lens is thick in the middle and thin at the edges. It pulls light rays to one point. This is why a <mark>magnifying glass</mark> makes small things look big.\n\nA concave lens is thin in the middle, so it spreads light out. Our eyes use a lens to focus pictures, too. Glasses add a lens to help us see well. So light bends, and lenses put that bending to work.",
    "illustration": "assets/illustrations/venus/2026-07/06.jpg",
    "illustration_caption": "A straw looking broken in water, and a convex lens pulling light rays to one point.",
    "glossary": [
      {
        "term": "refraction",
        "ko": "굴절 (빛이 다른 물질로 들어갈 때 휘는 현상)"
      },
      {
        "term": "convex",
        "ko": "볼록한 (가운데가 두꺼운)"
      },
      {
        "term": "concave",
        "ko": "오목한 (가운데가 얇은)"
      }
    ]
  },
